---
// Lesson 1.5 — Movement II: Speed, deltaTime & Tuning
// Evaluating multiple ways to move in Unity (Translate vs MovePosition vs
// linearVelocity vs AddForce), understanding Time.deltaTime for frame
// independence, and live tuning in the Inspector.
// Framed around the discovery from 1.4: transform.Translate walks through walls.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.5 — Movement II: Speed, deltaTime &amp; Tuning</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your character moves — but it walks straight through walls, and it does not yet
    <em>feel</em> like anything. Today you take both problems seriously. You will compare
    every way Unity offers to move an object, learn why <code>Time.deltaTime</code> is
    what makes speed fair across machines, and tune your character until it feels right.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        In Unity there is rarely just one way to solve a problem. Each of these four movement
        techniques exists because it solves a problem the others handle badly. Read the
        trade-offs — including which ones demand a component your character does not have yet:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>How it works</th><th>Needs a Rigidbody2D?</th><th>Best for</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>transform.Translate(vector)</code></td>
            <td>Directly offsets the object's coordinates. Skips the physics engine entirely.</td>
            <td><strong>No</strong></td>
            <td>Simple top-down games, moving UI, linear projectiles, bullets without gravity</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Rigidbody2D.MovePosition(position)</code></td>
            <td>Sweeps the physics body to a target position, stopping cleanly against solid colliders.</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>Kinematic characters, moving platforms, top-down games that must never clip into walls</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity = vector</code></td>
            <td>Assigns a velocity vector to the physics body. The physics engine does the moving.</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>Snappy platformers, arcade physics, carrying momentum across surfaces</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Rigidbody2D.AddForce(vector)</code></td>
            <td>Applies a physical push or impulse, factoring in mass, drag, and inertia.</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
            <td>Jumping, thrusters, ice physics, knockback from explosions or enemy hits</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Note what <code>MovePosition</code> buys you: it <em>sweeps</em> the body, meaning it
        checks the path rather than teleporting. That is the difference between walking into a
        wall and walking through one.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Choosing the Right Tool for the Job</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>You proved in <a href="1.4.html">1.4</a> that <code>transform.Translate</code> walks through walls. In your own words, why does <code>MovePosition</code> not?</li>
            <li>Look at the third column. Three of these four options need a component your Player does not have yet. What would you have to add to your character, and where would you add it — on the Player, or on the child Sprite?</li>
            <li>Why would a game about a spaceship drifting through space prefer <code>AddForce</code> over <code>Translate</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>The real question.</strong> This game is a top-down overworld where you explore, collect things, and later take damage from hazards. Which of these four does <em>this</em> game actually need? Argue for one, and name what you give up by choosing it.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Examine how each function and language feature behaves in isolation, across completely
        different systems:
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — Fallback defaults versus Inspector overrides, on a racing kart:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Code sets a fallback default, but whatever number is typed into the Inspector wins
[SerializeField] private float topSpeedMph = 45.0f;</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — Frame-rate independence, on a grandfather clock pendulum:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Multiplying a rate by Time.deltaTime guarantees 30 degrees per SECOND,
// whether this runs on a 30 FPS phone or a 240 FPS gaming PC
float degreesThisFrame = swingSpeedDegreesPerSecond * Time.deltaTime;</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — Four movement signatures compared:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// 1. Direct coordinate offset (arcade / bullets / moving UI):
transform.Translate(new Vector3(stepDistance, 0, 0));

// 2. Kinematic sweep (stops cleanly against walls, never penetrates):
rb2d.MovePosition(rb2d.position + stepVector);

// 3. Velocity assignment (arcade platformers):
rb2d.linearVelocity = new Vector2(runSpeed, rb2d.linearVelocity.y);

// 4. Force impulse (thrusters / a jump launch):
rb2d.AddForce(Vector2.up * launchThrust, ForceMode2D.Impulse);</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>A designer types <code>60.0</code> into the Inspector, but the C# file still says <code>= 45.0f</code>. Which number does the game use? What does that tell you about who is really in charge of a serialized field?</li>
            <li>Why does <code>transform.Translate</code> take a <em>relative</em> displacement ("how far to step right now"), while <code>MovePosition</code> takes an <em>absolute</em> target coordinate in world space?</li>
            <li>In signature 3, why does the code preserve <code>rb2d.linearVelocity.y</code> instead of simply setting both components? What would break if it zeroed them both?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Live-Tuning Workflow</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Numbers you guess at in the editor are almost never the numbers that feel right in
        motion. Professional designers tune <em>while the game runs</em>. Here is the workflow
        you will use for the rest of the year:
    </p>
    <ol>
        <li>Press Play in Unity, then walk around the overworld.</li>
        <li>While the game is still running, select the Player in the Hierarchy and change your speed knob in the Inspector. Walk again. Too sluggish?</li>
        <li>Try a much larger number. Notice how twitchy and uncontrollable it becomes.</li>
        <li>Bisect your way to a number that feels right for exploring a world this size. Try somewhere between 4 and 7 as a starting point.</li>
        <li><strong>Write the number down, exit Play mode</strong>, and set that value in edit mode so it persists.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Remember the play-mode rule from <a href="1.2.html">1.2</a>:</strong> any value
        you change while the Play button is blue resets when you stop the game. Use play mode
        to <em>find</em> your number, then enter it in edit mode to <em>keep</em> it.
    </div>

    <p>
        While you are tuning, also settle the design question you argued about in the Socratic
        section. If you decided to add a physics body, do it now — add the component in edit
        mode, then re-tune, because a physics-driven character will not feel the same as a
        translated one. Either choice is defensible. What matters is that you made it on
        purpose.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The deltaTime Abstraction</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Temporarily delete <code>* Time.deltaTime</code> from your movement calculation in <code>Update()</code>. Run the game and tap a direction key. Describe what happens in a single frame.</li>
            <li>A game runs at 60 FPS on your laptop and 240 FPS on a gaming desktop. Without <code>Time.deltaTime</code>, how much farther does the character travel per second on the faster machine?</li>
            <li>Why is <code>Time.deltaTime</code> best described as an <strong>abstraction</strong>? What messy detail is it hiding from you, and why is hiding it valuable?</li>
            <li>Set one speed knob far higher than the other — say 30 and 5 — and move diagonally. What does the character's path look like, and why?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Asymmetric movement &amp; feel</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Make your vertical speed a little slower than your horizontal speed. Walk around. In top-down 2D games, why do many classic RPGs do exactly this?</li>
            <li>Hold two direction keys to move diagonally. If both speeds are 5, the Pythagorean theorem says your diagonal speed is about 7.07 — you are moving 41% faster than intended. How could <code>vector.normalized</code> keep the speed constant in every direction, and what does "normalized" mean about the vector's length?</li>
            <li>If you chose a physics-based method, compare it against <code>transform.Translate</code> with the same speed value. Do they <em>feel</em> the same? If not, why not — what is the physics method doing that Translate is not?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Compare four ways to move an object in Unity: <code>Translate</code>, <code>MovePosition</code>, <code>linearVelocity</code>, <code>AddForce</code></li>
            <li>Say which of the four need a Rigidbody2D, and explain what a sweep does differently from a teleport</li>
            <li>Justify the movement method you chose for this game, and name its cost</li>
            <li>Explain how <code>Time.deltaTime</code> produces the same real-world speed at different frame rates</li>
            <li>Explain the relationship between a code default and a serialized Inspector value</li>
            <li>Save your tuned speeds and commit your changes</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — speed, vectors, and physics functions</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — Rigidbody2D and Transform APIs</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="1.6.html">1.6 — Animation States: IsWalking, WalkDir, Attack</a></p>
</div>`;
